{
 "schema": "targets/1",
 "note": "One entry per object. 'codes': every catalogue code that is the SAME object (M 31 and NGC 224 are one Andromeda). 'aliases': the other names you use for it. How to edit safely: (1) a plain-text editor (Notepad on the PC; on the Mac TextEdit only in plain-text mode with smart quotes off): one curly quote breaks the file, and then nothing resolves. (2) To add a name, put it in an entry's aliases: \"aliases\": [\"Wizard\", \"Wizard Nebula\"]. To add an object, copy a whole { ... } entry, paste it after another one, give it a new id. Straight double quotes only, a comma between entries, NO comma after the last one in a list, no comments. (3) Never change or reuse an id: your open/complete choices are keyed by it; to merge two entries, move the codes and aliases onto the one you keep and delete the other. (4) Write codes like M 31, NGC 224, IC 1318A (letter joined on), Sh2-157, C 25, Barnard 33 (never B 33), LDN 1235, vdB 126, Ced 214, Mel 22, HIP 106890, no leading zeros. (5) A code, a name or an alias may sit on only one entry; two entries with the same name make that name match neither (--targets-draft warns). (6) A name that contains a catalogue code always files by the code, whatever the words say. (7) Each computer has its own copy of this file: make the same change on both. (8) An update replaces this file by itself while you haven't edited it; if you have, it leaves targets.json.new beside it: merge the two by hand. Then run --targets-draft: it checks the file and says what it still doesn't know. Optional per target, read by the Observatory page: 'type' (Galaxy, Emission nebula, Open cluster...), 'constellation', 'ra' (hours, decimal) and 'dec' (degrees, decimal). A target without ra/dec is listed but not drawn on the sky map. The first set came from the Astro Archive Observatory's catalogue (29 Aug 2026).",
 "review": "Seed built 10 Oct 2026 from the importer's own name list plus a merge pass; 37 targets added 12 Oct from the Mac's --targets-draft (48 names; all resolve except 'Unknown', 3 frames, no object). For Brett: 'NGC 1893 - Flaming Star' files under NGC 1893 (the Tadpoles' cluster, IC 410) because the code wins, not under IC 405; Fish on a Platter is Barnard 144; Middle Heart (LDN 1373) is the Heart's own core; Markarian's Chain, the Milky Way core and the comet are name-only. Kept apart on purpose until Brett says: Sh2-171 (Teddy Bear) and NGC 7822; NGC 6910 (the cluster) and IC 1318 (the Sadr nebula); NGC 6823 and NGC 6820 / Sh2-86; NGC 1893 and IC 410.",
